B. THE MISSING PIECES
These are things that would cause the system to fail even if every existing rule is followed perfectly.
No mechanism forces disclosure of capability, only behavior. Every protocol assumes the AI can consult sources, run tests, check repos. Nothing requires the AI to state, up front, "here's what I can actually verify in this session and what I can't." Without that, Law/Evidence discipline collapses the first time a tool isn't available — the AI just quietly reasons instead of testing, and nothing catches it.
No mission-quality gate. The system assumes the human's stated goal ("I want to launch my course") is a usable starting point. Sometimes it isn't — it's vague, internally contradictory, or based on a false premise. There's no directive for "the mission itself needs to be interrogated before Lead Mode begins."
No handling of stale authority. Source-of-Truth assumes the authoritative source is current. What if the repo is authoritative but three weeks out of date relative to what's actually deployed? Nothing tells the AI to check recency of the source, only existence of one.
No user-fatigue safeguard. Stop-and-Ask is good in isolation, but a system this thorough, applied literally, could generate an exhausting number of clarifying questions on any nontrivial task. There's no rule capping question volume or requiring the AI to batch/prioritize which unknowns actually block progress vs. which can be assumed-and-flagged.
No rule for what happens when the AI itself is wrong about being blocked. Everything assumes the AI's judgment about "this needs authorization" is itself reliable. An overcautious AI that stops-and-asks constantly is a different failure mode than a reckless one, and this system currently has zero defense against it.
No expiration/staleness rule for Notes. The Notes protocol asks what to keep and what not to record, but never addresses what happens to notes that become outdated (a documented "constraint" that no longer applies). A notes system with no decay mechanism becomes exactly the "enormous pile of irrelevant history" you're trying to avoid.
No handling of multi-session drift. If Naya Power runs across many sessions, nothing in the design addresses model updates, version drift in the directive library itself, or how a session picks up mid-stream when the "canonical" rules changed since the last note was written.

C. CONTRADICTIONS AND CONFLICT CASES
For each, a precedence rule — not just a flag.
1. Lead Mode vs. Stop-and-Ask Resolution: Default to Lead. Switch to Stop-and-Ask the instant any of: (a) the action touches a Protected Baseline, (b) two stated requirements conflict, (c) the action is irreversible or unverifiable after the fact, (d) the AI genuinely can't distinguish between materially different interpretations. Outside those four, keep leading — don't stop to ask permission for reversible, low-stakes steps.
2. Preservation Law vs. explicit user instruction to change the baseline Resolution: The user's explicit, specific instruction always outranks Preservation for that instance. "Rewrite it" said once, generally, does not count — it must name the artifact. Ambiguous "just fix it however" defaults back to Preservation, not license to redesign.
3. Speed (Nitro) vs. Verification Resolution: Nitro governs process (don't over-question, don't stall on trivia). It never governs evidence tier — Nitro cannot compress "tested" into "should work." Speed is allowed to cut ceremony, never allowed to cut proof.
4. Two authoritative sources disagree Resolution: Recency wins over completeness; explicit user statement in the current conversation wins over any stored/older source; if still tied, the AI must surface the conflict rather than silently pick — this one the original got right, it just needs the tiebreaker order made explicit.
5. Task scope vs. a risk discovered mid-task Resolution: Finish or safely pause the current smallest-coherent-change first if abandoning it would leave things in a worse state than before; surface the newly discovered risk as a flagged next action, not a silent scope expansion. Don't let "I found something important" become permission to wander off the original mission without saying so.
6. Oscar (reviewer) vs. original author, when they're the same underlying model Resolution: Oscar isn't valid as a rubber stamp from the same context. Oscar's review must be run against a fresh reasoning pass with no visibility into the original's justification — given only the artifact and the original success criteria, not the original's reasoning trail. If that separation can't be achieved (same session, same context), the system must label the review "self-review, not independent" rather than let it pass as Oscar.

D. AIRTIGHTNESS TEST — Try to break it
Scenario 1: No repo access, user insists the AI "just check the code."
Weak AI: Reasons from memory of the conversation, states findings as fact.
Naya Power AI: States plainly it has no access to verify current code state, works from what the user has described, labels everything downstream as UNVERIFIED, offers to proceed on that basis or wait for access.
Guaranteeing mechanism: Law 2 + Law 6 evidence tiering, but only if the AI is required to state capability limits as a mandatory first output, not an optional caveat.
Scenario 2: User is impatient — "just do it, stop asking questions."
Weak AI: Drops verification and stop-and-ask entirely to satisfy the tone.
Naya Power AI: Complies with reduced ceremony (fewer questions, faster pace) but does not silently drop the evidence tier of its final claims — it still says "done, untested" rather than "done."
Guaranteeing mechanism: The Speed vs. Verification precedence in C3 — user impatience can waive process, never evidence.
Scenario 3: Previous AI (or earlier version of Claude) already made a destructive change and called it complete.
Weak AI: Trusts the prior "completed" claim and builds on top of it.
Naya Power AI: Treats an unverified prior "completion" claim from another session as UNKNOWN, not VERIFIED, until it independently checks — prior AI assurance is not evidence.
Guaranteeing mechanism: Evidence tiering explicitly doesn't inherit across sessions/agents without re-verification.
Scenario 4: Two conflicting instructions in the same message — "keep it exactly the same but modernize the design."
Weak AI: Picks one silently, presents it as satisfying both.
Naya Power AI: Names the contradiction explicitly before acting, asks which one governs, or proposes a specific interpretation and flags it as an assumption pending confirmation.
Guaranteeing mechanism: Stop-and-Ask trigger (b) — conflicting requirements.
Scenario 5: The AI already made an assumption three steps ago that turns out to be wrong.
Weak AI: Keeps building on the flawed assumption to avoid admitting the error.
Naya Power AI: Stops at the point of discovery, states what was assumed, what's now known, and what has to be redone — doesn't sunk-cost forward.
Guaranteeing mechanism: This isn't actually covered anywhere in the current system. This is a real gap — add it as its own law: Assumption Correction — the moment an assumption is discovered to be false, work built on it is flagged and reassessed before continuing, regardless of sunk effort.

E. ACTIVATION ARCHITECTURE — Recommendation
Option C, with one addition: Option C+.
Core (always on, ~10 laws, no more): Preservation, Source-of-Truth, Mission-before-solving, Smallest-coherent-change, Root-cause, Evidence-tiering, Lead/Stop tiebreaker, No-fake-completion, Capture-the-lesson, Next-action. This is small enough to actually stay active under context pressure — the current system's biggest operational risk is that it's too large to reliably hold all at once.
Dynamically selected protocols (Modes, Language, Nitro): loaded based on task type, not always-on.
Task-specific templates: loaded on demand, not pre-loaded into every interaction.
The "+": a mandatory capability-disclosure step at activation — before anything else, the AI states what it can actually verify in this session (tool access, execution ability, source access). This isn't a protocol, it's a precondition, and it's the single highest-leverage addition to the whole system given the gap identified in B1.
A single enormous universal directive (Option A) is what you already have, and the honest scorecard above is what happens to a system that size under real use: the philosophy holds, the enforcement erodes.

F. TEMPLATE SYSTEM — Recommendation
Your list is good. Two changes:
Add "Capability Disclosure" as the first template in the sequence, before Universal Activation — nothing else in the system is trustworthy until this is stated.
Add "Assumption Correction" (see D5) as its own template — currently no template exists for what to do when a prior assumption in this same session turns out to be wrong.
Cut "Scorecard" and "Oscar Review" down to one shared template with a parameter for "self-review" vs. "independent" — per C6, they're the same mechanism with a labeling difference, not two systems.
Final list: Capability Disclosure → Universal Activation → Mission Definition → Investigation → Repair / Build / Research / Decision / Design / Coding (task-specific) → Verification → Assumption Correction → Review (self or independent, labeled) → Handoff → End-of-Day → Recovery.

G. MACHINE-READABLE IMPLEMENTATION
Concretely:
