Each law/template gets: an ID, a version, a numeric priority (for precedence resolution per C), required inputs, prohibited actions, an explicit override condition, and — critically — a machine-checkable evidence requirement where applicable, so "verified" isn't just a word the AI is trusted to use correctly but a field that has to be populated with something before the claim is allowed to render.
This is the actual difference between "inspirational" and "operational": inspirational systems are prose the AI is supposed to remember to apply. Operational systems are structured objects the AI has to fill in before it's allowed to proceed — that's a UX/prompting difference, not a technical one, but it's the whole ballgame.

H. THE UNIVERSAL HUMAN EXPERIENCE
Ordinary person, no prompting skill, says: "I want to launch my course."
Capability disclosure (invisible to user unless relevant): the AI silently checks what it can access.
Mission interrogation, minimal friction: "Got it — a few quick things so I get this right: what's the course, and do you have a target date?" (Two questions max at this stage, not twelve.)
AI states back the mission in plain language and asks for one confirmation: "Here's what I understand — correct?"
AI leads: proposes the plan, flags the two or three things it's genuinely unsure about, proceeds on everything else.
At each meaningful milestone, states plainly what's done, what's confirmed vs. assumed, and what's next — in the user's language, not systems language.
At the end, a short, human-readable summary: what got built, what to double check, what's next. No jargon, no exposed internal template structure.
The entire architecture above should be invisible to this person. If they ever see the word "Protected Baseline" in a normal interaction, the UX has failed — the rigor should produce a better feeling interaction (fewer surprises, fewer redone mistakes), not a more complicated one.

MOST IMPORTANT QUESTION — Honest answer
If I were building this from scratch, I would build less of it, not more.
The real value in Naya Power is about four ideas: don't destroy working things without permission, don't claim certainty you don't have, find the actual cause instead of the loudest symptom, and always leave the human with a next step. Those four, actually enforced, would move the needle more than the other thirty-eight sections combined.
The risk in the current direction is that the system is optimizing for completeness of philosophy when the actual bottleneck is enforcement under pressure — an AI that has read ten protocols and forty rules but has no structural mechanism forcing it to disclose capability limits, state evidence tiers explicitly, or catch its own bad assumptions mid-stream will still fail in exactly the ways this document is trying to prevent. It'll just fail more articulately, having quoted the right law right before violating it.
So: don't add an eleventh protocol. Take the four ideas above, make them genuinely mechanical (not prose the AI is trusted to remember, but structured steps it has to fill in before it's allowed to say "done"), and get those working reliably before expanding scope. A small system that's actually enforced beats a comprehensive one that's aspirational — which is, not coincidentally, exactly what Law 1 of this document already says about a Protected Baseline. Apply that same discipline to the document itself.
